(11)**, Rust died from **SIGABRT (6)** — `rustc` injects a null-pointer check before every raw-pointer deref under `-C debug-assertions`, and `slice::from_raw_parts` adds its own precondition assert. Fixed on the Rust side by routing the byte loads through a `load_u8` helper that does a one-byte libc `memcpy` — exactly the plain load the C compiler emits, with no injected checks. One byte at a time also guarantees the translation never touches a byte C wouldn't (verified by a page-boundary test where the input ends exactly at an unmapped page).\n\nRows 8 and 9 now assert the **same signal** in forked children, not merely \"both failed\".\n\n## Two things I did to avoid a false pass\n\n- **Mutation check** (`./mutation_check.sh`): injected 18 plausible translation bugs one at a time — **18/18 caught, 0 survivors**, including `null deref instead of memcpy load` and `over-read: load 8 bytes for the tail`, which prove the two fixes above are load-bearing. The sign-extension quirks are asserted *positively* (row 6 proves lengths 4–7 **collide** when `d[3] >= 0x80`; row 7 proves `d[4..8]` are **ignored**), so a Rust that \"fixed\" the C's integer promotion fails.\n- **C UB stability**: the C depends on signed-overflow UB (`d[3] << 24`), so I compiled `lib.c` at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os` (outside `c_src/`, which is untouched — md5 unchanged) and ran the whole suite against each × both Rust profiles: **10/10 pass**.\n\nReproduce with `OPT_SWEEP=1 ./verify_all.sh`; `VERIFICATION.md` records the completion gate."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1178,"cache_read_input_tokens":149427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1178,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bffa5c02-7db2-4d23-8358-5f74f3f0e8e3","uuid":"59ca0893-8fa3-4ccb-b61f-72de12a2dedf","timestamp":"2026-08-20T18:45:26.360Z"}
